// Cloudflare deployment for the PORTAL — static assets, no Worker script.
//
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// WHY THERE IS NO `main`
//
// The portal is an Astro static build (`output: "static"` in
// astro.config.mjs): `npm run build` emits plain HTML, CSS and JS into
// `dist/` and there is no request-time server. Wrangler's default assumption
// is a script Worker, so without this file `wrangler versions upload` fails
// with "Worker's entry point not specified" — it was looking for code that
// does not exist rather than for a directory to serve.
//
// `assets.directory` is the whole deployment. Adding a `main` here would mean
// inventing a Worker the portal does not have.
//
// WHAT THIS CONFIG DOES **NOT** DEPLOY
//
//   The module APIs. `database`, `ai` and `scrapers` each have their own
//   config and are deployed explicitly:
//       npx wrangler deploy --config deploy/database/wrangler.toml
//   Being in this repo does not put them on this Worker; only `dist/` ships.
//
//   The admin panel. `deploy/admin/` is Node-only (`node:http`), holds
//   `ADMIN_API_TOKEN` in its environment, and must stay on the private LXC
//   host. It is not part of the Astro build, so it cannot reach `dist/` — and
//   it must never be given a public Cloudflare route. See deploy/README.md
//   § Admin panel.
//
// BUILD SETTINGS (Cloudflare dashboard → Workers → Builds)
//   Build command:   npm run build
//   Deploy command:  npx wrangler deploy          (production)
//                    npx wrangler versions upload (preview branches)
//   Both read this file; neither needs a path argument once `assets` is set.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
{
  "name": "silicon-index",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-01",

  "assets": {
    "directory": "./dist",

    // Astro builds directory-style routes (`dist/admin/index.html`), so
    // `/admin` and `/admin/` must both resolve to that file.
    "html_handling": "auto-trailing-slash",

    // There is no `src/pages/404.astro`, so there is no `dist/404.html` to
    // point at. Cloudflare's own 404 is served instead. Add a 404 page first
    // if you want this switched to "404-page" — naming a file that is not in
    // the build is how a deploy starts serving blank pages.
    "not_found_handling": "none"
  }
}
